<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fiji</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fiji Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fiji is an island country in the south-western Pacific, made up of over 300 islands, chief among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-levu">Vanua Levu</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>. Rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> such as Bau dominated it until Seru Cakobau ceded the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874. Indian indentured labourers brought to work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations from 1879 formed a large community. Fiji became independent in 1970 and has since experienced several coups.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fiji.jpg" alt="Fiji" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tessa Mackenzie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Fiji.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indian indentured labourers and soldiers carried communities across the empire, to <b>Fiji</b>, Mauritius, the Caribbean and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tupou I also carried Tongan power abroad: in 1855 he sailed to <b>Fiji</b> with a large force and won the Battle of Kaba for his ally Cakobau of Bau, while his kinsman Enele Maʻafu built up a Tongan-led domain in the Lau islands of eastern Fiji.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Geochemical study of stone tools from the royal centres has shown that about two-thirds were long-distance imports, mostly from Samoa and <b>Fiji</b>, with one adze from the Society Islands some 2,500 kilometres away, and that such contacts intensified after about 1200.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Indentured labourers taken from India by the British in the 19th century founded Hindu communities in Mauritius, <b>Fiji</b>, Trinidad, Guyana and South Africa, and migration in the 20th century carried Hinduism to Britain, North America and the Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Migrants of the colonial era took it to Malaysia, Singapore, Mauritius, South Africa, Burma and other lands; in Réunion, Guyana, <b>Fiji</b> and Trinidad many have Tamil origins but few speak it, and in Réunion, where France forbade it in public, it is now being learnt again.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>From the Bismarcks, Lapita voyagers passed by the main Solomon Islands, long inhabited, where few Lapita sites are known, and became the first people of the Reef and Santa Cruz Islands, Vanuatu, New Caledonia, <b>Fiji</b>, Tonga and Samoa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
